          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Teichm%C3%BCller+theory"} Teichmüller theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            has been called
            %em inter-universal Teichmüller theory
            (often abbreviated
            = succeed ")" do
              %em IUTT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shinichi+Mochizuki"} Shinichi Mochizuki
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anabelian+geometry"} anabelian geometry
          %p#InterUniversal The term “Inter-Universal” refers to Mochizuki’s Key Principle of Inter-Universality [IUTT I, Section I3, Page 25-26] which requires one to work with arbitrary geometric base-points for the tempered fundamental groups.
          %p A choice of a geometric base-point for the tempered fundamental group corresponds to a choice of a fiber functor for the galois category of tempered coverings. Hence Mochizuki asserts [IUTT I, Section I3 Page 25-26] that the idea of working with distinct geometric base-points is tantamount to relating the distinct set-theoretic universes the fiber functors arise from and so theory is referred to as being inter-universal. [The fundamental group is, of course, independent (or agnostic) of the choice of the fiber functor, i.e. it is an object which is oblivious to the choice of the set theoretic universe it arises from.]
          %p
            Hence Mochizuki has asserted that this theory is meant to formulated explicitly in a way that respects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+enlargement"} universe enlargement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+polymorphism"} universe polymorphic
            (
            %a{:href => "#IUTTIV"}> IUTT IV, remark 3.1.4
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Yamashita13"} Yamashita 13
          %p
            While [IUTT I-IV] offers scant mathematical justification of the Teichmuller aspects of the theory (i.e. [IUTT I-IV] provides no mathematical justification why the theory is a Teichmuller Theory), a clearest proof of this Teichmuller aspect has been provided (in all dimensions) in the works of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirti+Joshi"} Kirti Joshi
          %p
            It is claimed (
            %a{:href => "#IUTTIV"}> IUTT IV
            ) that a proof of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abc+conjecture"} abc conjecture
            can be given in IUTT. In 2018, a document
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/why_abc_is_still_a_conjecture.pdf", :title => "Why _abc_ is still a conjecture"}
              Why
              %em abc
              is still a conjecture
            was written by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Stix"} Jakob Stix
            raising objections to the argument. More accurate critiques have appeared as a part of the works of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirti+Joshi"} Kirti Joshi
            on the theory of Arithmetic Teichmuller Spaces which includes, in dimension one and genus one, a precise version of Mochizuki’s IUTT.
          %p
            In brief, the novel idea due to Mochizuki, is to average over deformations of arithmetic (or an arithmetic holomorphic structure) itself (this notion has been precisely quantified in the works of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirti+Joshi"} Kirti Joshi
          %h2#details Details
          %h3#pilot_objects Pilot objects
          %p
            Important to the arguments in the latter parts of the IUTT series are what Mochizuki refers to as pilot objects. (A precise construction of Pilot objects has been independently provided in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirti+Joshi"} Construction of Arithmetic Teichmuller Spaces III: A Rosetta Stone and a proof of Mochizuki’s Corollary 3.12
          %p
            Though the terminology in
            %a{:href => "#IUTTIII"} IUTT III
            is very dense, Mochizuki explains in Example 3.6, Remark 3.6.1, and subsequently that these can be understood in quite elementary terms, which we now describe.
          %p
            Let
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
